{"t_ms": 7650, "stream": "stdout", "data": {"event": "result", "result": {"conversation_id": "956514b0-986c-4168-8130-c6251328d6fb", "status": "SUCCESS", "response": "### The Indivisible Atoms of Arithmetic\n\nIn the vast landscape of mathematics, prime numbers stand as fundamental building blocks—the indivisible atoms of arithmetic. Defined simply as integers greater than one that are divisible only by one and themselves, primes possess an elemental purity. Yet beneath this deceptive simplicity lies one of the deepest, most beautiful mysteries in human inquiry: an unending dance between order and chaos, predictability and randomness.\n\nThe fascination with primes stretches back to antiquity. In approximately 300 BCE, the ancient Greek mathematician Euclid formulated one of history’s most elegant proofs in his *Elements*, demonstrating that the supply of prime numbers is infinite. If one assumes a finite list of primes, multiplying them together and adding one yields a new number that is either prime itself or divisible by a prime not on the original list. Euclid’s proof established an eternal truth: no matter how far we travel along the number line, new primes will continue to emerge.\n\nDespite their infinite nature, primes become increasingly sparse as numbers grow larger. For centuries, their distribution appeared entirely erratic, defying simple formulas. It was not until the nineteenth century that Carl Friedrich Gauss and Adrien-Marie Legendre conjectured the Prime Number Theorem, later proven in 1896 by Jacques Hadamard and Charles Jean de la Vallée Poussin. The theorem revealed a breathtaking macroscopic regularity: the probability that a randomly chosen integer $N$ is prime is inversely proportional to its natural logarithm, $\\ln(N)$. While individual primes appear almost randomly, their collective behavior follows a remarkably smooth logarithmic curve.\n\nThis duality between local chaos and global harmony is epitomized by the Riemann Hypothesis, proposed by Bernhard Riemann in 1859. By extending the zeta function into the complex plane, Riemann connected the distribution of primes directly to the non-trivial zeros of this function. Widely regarded as the most famous unsolved problem in mathematics, the Riemann Hypothesis asserts that all these non-trivial zeros lie on a single critical line. If true, it would unlock a profound harmonic symphony governing the exact fluctuations of prime numbers.\n\nBeyond their aesthetic allure in pure mathematics, prime numbers have quietly become the bedrock of modern technological civilization. In the 1970s, the development of asymmetric public-key cryptography—most notably the RSA algorithm—transformed prime numbers into indispensable digital guardians. The security of global commerce, secure communication, and online privacy rests upon a computational asymmetry: multiplying two large prime numbers together is nearly instantaneous, but factoring their astronomical product back into its constituent primes is virtually impossible with classical computing resources.\n\nPrime numbers represent a sublime intersection of philosophy, abstract theory, and practical utility. They remind us that the most basic structures in nature often harbor the deepest questions. As mathematicians continue to explore unresolved enigmas like the Twin Prime Conjecture or the Goldbach Conjecture, primes remain an enduring testament to the infinite richness waiting to be uncovered within the simplest concepts.\n", "duration_seconds": 6.674385393, "num_turns": 1, "usage": {"input_tokens": 11252, "output_tokens": 639, "thinking_tokens": 0, "cache_read_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 11891}}}}
